<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco Goya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1746">1746</a>–<a href="/years/1828">1828</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Francisco José de Goya y Lucientes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco Goya was a Spanish romantic painter and printmaker, often called the last of the Old Masters and the first of the moderns. Born in Fuendetodos, Aragon, he became a court painter to the Spanish Crown in 1786 and in 1799 Primer Pintor de Cámara, the highest rank for a Spanish court painter. An illness in 1793 left him deaf, and his work grew darker, from the Caprichos and the Disasters of War prints to The Third of May 1808 and the Black Paintings of 1819–1823 on the walls of his house, the Quinta del Sordo. He left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1824 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, where he died in 1828.</p></div>
